{"name":"Chivalrous Walrus","hex":"#816558","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chivalrous","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Honourable, especially to women; gallant.","Involving chivalry."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chivalrous"},{"term":"walrus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large Arctic pinniped having long tusks, tough, wrinkled skin, and four flippers, Odobenus rosmarus.","A man with a walrus moustache."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hunt walruses","To be like a walrus\nTo move dragging one's belly along the floor\n\nTo hang like a walrus's moustache\n\nTo be prominent, like tusks"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/walrus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chivalry","blocks":[{"text":"Chivalry, or the chivalric language, is an informal and varying code of conduct that developed in France between 1170 and 1220. It is associated with the medieval Christian institution of knighthood, with knights being members of various chivalric orders, and with knights' and gentlemen's behaviours which were governed by chivalrous social codes. The ideals of chivalry were popularized in medieval literature, particularly the literary cycles known as the Matter of France, relating to the legendary companions of Charlemagne and his men-at-arms, the paladins, and the Matter of Britain, informed by Geoffrey of Monmouth's Historia Regum Britanniae, written in the 1130s, which popularized the legend of King Arthur and his knights of the Round Table."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Meister_der_Manessischen_Liederhandschrift_001.jpg/330px-Meister_der_Manessischen_Liederhandschrift_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chivalry"},{"title":"Walrus","blocks":[{"text":"The walrus is a large pinniped marine mammal with discontinuous distribution about the North Pole in the Arctic Ocean and subarctic seas of the Northern Hemisphere. It is the only extant species in the family Odobenidae and genus Odobenus. This species is subdivided into two subspecies: the Atlantic walrus, which lives in the Atlantic Ocean, and the Pacific walrus, which lives in the Pacific Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Walrus_in_the_Russian_Arctic_National_Park%2C_Novaya_Zemlya_2015-2.jpg/330px-Walrus_in_the_Russian_Arctic_National_Park%2C_Novaya_Zemlya_2015-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walrus"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Leighton-God_Speed%21.jpg/500px-Leighton-God_Speed%21.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Leighton-God_Speed%21.jpg/1280px-Leighton-God_Speed%21.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Leighton-God_Speed%21.jpg/500px-Leighton-God_Speed%21.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"God Speed by English artist Edmund Leighton, 1900: depicting an armoured knight departing for war and leaving his beloved","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeighton-God_Speed!.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Roman_Museum_007.jpg/500px-Roman_Museum_007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Roman_Museum_007.jpg/1280px-Roman_Museum_007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Roman_Museum_007.jpg/500px-Roman_Museum_007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Reconstruction of a Roman cavalryman